WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.149
How's everybody doing?

00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:15.725
Is everybody really pumped up to
talk about enterprise software?

00:00:15.725 --> 00:00:17.478
Good, that's what you want to do today.

00:00:17.478 --> 00:00:22.271
So I'm Aaron Levie CEO, co-founder of Box.

00:00:22.271 --> 00:00:27.703
And what I wanted to do was share
a little bit about our story at Box,

00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:31.170
and how we started building our company.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.913
We actually didn't start as
an enterprise software company,

00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:36.356
we eventually changed our
business model and our path.

00:00:36.356 --> 00:00:40.284
But I want to talk a little bit about
how we got to that decision, and

00:00:40.284 --> 00:00:43.658
how we started building up our product and
our company.

00:00:43.658 --> 00:00:47.608
And then, if, and I really hope this
is true, if many of you go out and

00:00:47.608 --> 00:00:49.939
build enterprise software companies.

00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:54.257
Maybe there's some lessons that we've
learned that it took us a long time to

00:00:54.257 --> 00:00:57.291
learn, that maybe you can
learn a little bit faster,

00:00:57.291 --> 00:01:00.490
because of some of the things
that we've gone through.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:04.410
So and I think that with some time,
we can open up for questions or what not.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.250
So is that all cool?

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.378
Agree with that?
Okay, this is going to be highly

00:01:07.378 --> 00:01:09.960
interactive, so we've gotta talk to
each other, you know what I'm saying?

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.569
&gt;&gt; Gotcha.
&gt;&gt; Good, okay.

00:01:12.569 --> 00:01:14.845
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I love it when the least interactive

00:01:14.845 --> 00:01:16.962
part is when I call out that
we need to be interactive.

00:01:16.962 --> 00:01:20.454
But okay, so
here's how we have been building for

00:01:20.454 --> 00:01:25.540
the modern enterprise and maybe some
lessons that you can take with you.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:32.180
So we started Box in 2005,
and we were actually

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:35.870
sophomores and freshman in college at
the time when the idea got started.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.780
So this was about 12 years ago.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:43.460
And what was happening was everywhere
that we were working from,

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.950
everywhere that we were
trying to access files from,

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.500
anybody we were trying to share data with,
was just too hard to do this.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.101
So we had to have these USB thumb drives,

00:01:54.101 --> 00:01:57.540
hopefully you've never
had to use one before.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.490
You have USB thumb drives, you're emailing
yourself files, you have FTP accounts.

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:05.584
And it was just way too complicated for
just getting daily work done,

00:02:05.584 --> 00:02:07.780
just way too slow, too cumbersome.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.120
And at the time, I actually did an
internship with a company that also used

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.950
enterprise software, for sharing and
collaborating and managing content, and

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.460
that technology was also way
too complicated and complex.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.933
And so this is back in, really we
got the idea in kind of late 2004.

00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:28.135
And what was happening was we saw this
confluence of major technology trends,

00:02:28.135 --> 00:02:31.253
that we felt could go and
solve this problem of, why is it so

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:34.516
hard just to share files and
get work done with other people?

00:02:34.516 --> 00:02:38.996
And so we saw that there would be
a handful of major technology trends that

00:02:38.996 --> 00:02:43.550
could ultimately solve this problem of,
working with our information,

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.910
working with our data from anywhere.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:51.294
So the big three,
though that we focused in on, the first

00:02:51.294 --> 00:02:56.150
was mobile ubiquity, and
now obviously this is a standard trend.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:00.890
No software company would start without
a mobile strategy, but back in 2004 and

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:01.930
2005, it wasn't the case.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.739
But we had this vision, and we had this
foresight where we thought back in

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:08.363
2004 and 2005 that over the next decade,

00:03:08.363 --> 00:03:11.937
the entire planet would start to
be using Blackberrys everywhere.

00:03:11.937 --> 00:03:19.530
So that was our vision, and so we were
wrong, fortunately, we got better devices.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.180
But we knew that mobile was certainly
going to be a really powerful opportunity

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.899
in the future, and was going to change
the way people worked with information.

00:03:26.899 --> 00:03:30.713
So we're glad that it ended up being
a little bit of a better platform.

00:03:30.713 --> 00:03:34.286
We also saw this trend of
the cost of computing and

00:03:34.286 --> 00:03:37.960
the cost of storage was
dropping pretty rapidly.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.430
So it wasn't called cloud
computing at the time.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.750
But you could see that there was
this ability to buy lots and

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.170
lots of infrastructure at scale,
centralize it in one place, and

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.310
then be able to build applications
on it over the Internet.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.072
And so we sort of figure why is
it that people are going out and

00:03:53.072 --> 00:03:56.002
building their own infrastructure and
their own data center,

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:58.503
when a software provider
can do this all themselves?

00:03:58.503 --> 00:04:01.573
And get the economies of
scale to serve lots and

00:04:01.573 --> 00:04:04.870
lots of customers with that technology?

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:06.872
So we said okay, at some point in time,

00:04:06.872 --> 00:04:10.638
computing is going to reach this point
where it's really, really cheap and

00:04:10.638 --> 00:04:13.932
inexpensive for anybody, and so
thus it should be in the cloud.

00:04:13.932 --> 00:04:16.677
And then finally,
we figured that as more and

00:04:16.677 --> 00:04:19.430
more people were connected
to the Internet.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:22.144
As more and more people and businesses
were working on mobile devices, and

00:04:22.144 --> 00:04:25.045
collaborating more and more, that we
would become much more interconnected.

00:04:25.045 --> 00:04:29.373
And so businesses that once had to
have all of their own people, and

00:04:29.373 --> 00:04:33.797
infrastructure and competencies
within their own organization.

00:04:33.797 --> 00:04:37.315
Would eventually be able to be networked
with partners, and clients, and vendors

00:04:37.315 --> 00:04:40.600
and colleagues all around the world,
and be able to get value from everyone.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.960
Which would mean, that you'd need
software that would be better at

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.040
letting us interact with one
another over the Internet.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.935
As opposed to just people with
our own corporate network,

00:04:48.935 --> 00:04:52.371
which is where our market had
traditionally kind of been focused on.

00:04:52.371 --> 00:04:55.437
And so we took these three big trends,
everybody on the Internet,

00:04:55.437 --> 00:04:58.750
everybody on mobile and really,
really cheap computing and storage.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:03.470
And we said, what would we design if
those conditions were true in the future?

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.058
What would we design to build a really,
really simple way for

00:05:06.058 --> 00:05:08.765
people to share files and
securely access their information?

00:05:08.765 --> 00:05:12.110
And so we launched this thing, it was
actually called Box.net at the time.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.128
And we launched this site, and the idea
was really simple, you could upload your

00:05:18.128 --> 00:05:22.181
files, and you could access them from
any web browser, any mobile device.

00:05:22.181 --> 00:05:24.057
And within a few months of launching,

00:05:24.057 --> 00:05:26.440
thousands of people were signing up for
it.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.610
And we had worked on some mechanics to
make sure that was as simple as possible,

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.540
so we had way easier sign up flow.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.480
It was just focused on the end user,
so in traditional IT environments,

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.600
you usually had to go to corporate IT, or
you had go to corporate security to be

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.480
able to be able to get
access to technology.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:45.117
We just let anybody sign up with their
own information, their own email address,

00:05:45.117 --> 00:05:48.186
which was actually pretty disruptive
at the time to traditional IT.

00:05:48.186 --> 00:05:52.588
So anybody could go online, they could get
a couple gigabytes of storage space, and

00:05:52.588 --> 00:05:55.940
be able to share and
access their information from anywhere.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:58.190
And that was the original idea.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:02.535
And what we sort of ultimately realized,
that we actually had no clue about,

00:06:02.535 --> 00:06:04.560
because we were just in
college at the time.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.174
Was that enterprises all around the world,
and companies of all sizes,

00:06:09.174 --> 00:06:12.436
were just used to really,
really bad technology.

00:06:12.436 --> 00:06:15.972
And the bar was just really,
really low for what you could do.

00:06:15.972 --> 00:06:21.023
Our first application was
pretty horrendous, but

00:06:21.023 --> 00:06:27.846
the bar was so low that all you had
to do was solve some basic problems.

00:06:27.846 --> 00:06:29.012
And then end users within those IT
environments, within those enterprises,

00:06:29.012 --> 00:06:29.786
would all of a sudden want to
adopt your technology.

00:06:29.786 --> 00:06:33.161
And so what we discovered was that
people all around the world, and

00:06:33.161 --> 00:06:34.435
companies of all sizes.

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:38.586
Whether it was a Fortune 500 company that
was spending hundreds of millions of

00:06:38.586 --> 00:06:41.028
dollars on technology,
or small businesses,

00:06:41.028 --> 00:06:44.890
everybody had this interest and
demand for really, really simple tools.

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:48.733
So one of our competitors at the time,
the primary product you had to use for

00:06:48.733 --> 00:06:51.784
sharing information looked
a little something like this.

00:06:51.784 --> 00:06:54.671
This is a screenshot from
one of the products, and

00:06:54.671 --> 00:06:58.060
it's pretty horribly designed technology.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:01.193
I don't know even what it says,
it's just a screen grab from the Internet.

00:07:01.193 --> 00:07:05.263
But really not elegant software for
working with other people and

00:07:05.263 --> 00:07:06.730
collaborating.

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:11.769
And so what sort of started out for
us as a really simple way to share files,

00:07:11.769 --> 00:07:16.727
it was that dead simple of a proposition,
actually turned out that it was

00:07:16.727 --> 00:07:21.040
the sort of crux of how people
could be working in the future.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.447
And it was a much easier way for
people to be able to work.

00:07:23.447 --> 00:07:30.140
And so, we're getting a little security
prompt, of course that has to happen.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:34.312
Okay, so what started out as
a really simple way to share files,

00:07:34.312 --> 00:07:36.720
turned into this new way of working.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.816
And we weren't trying to solve this vast
problem of how can companies work in

00:07:40.816 --> 00:07:41.591
a modern way?

00:07:41.591 --> 00:07:45.508
All we were trying to do was build really
simple technology that makes it easy for

00:07:45.508 --> 00:07:48.560
people to share files and
access information from anywhere.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:49.921
And then we eventually
evolved the strategy.

00:07:49.921 --> 00:07:55.126
And things were growing so
quickly that we basically decided,

00:07:55.126 --> 00:08:01.250
like any classic start-up story,
we decided to get in a minivan

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.550
And drop out of college and move to
the Bay Area to go and build the company.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:10.080
And there's always this part in the story,
where if you kind of roll back in time, so

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:15.870
this was us living and
working out of a renovated garage.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:21.200
So that thing behind us is actually,
technically, actually zoned as a garage,

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.060
but we were living out of it, so
it was actually pretty illegal.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.940
But we were just the four founders,
just going out, and

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.400
we had all dropped out of college and
building at this.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.720
What we didn't yet fully now was this
enterprise software company that

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.330
eventually would become a whole
enterprise software platform.

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.104
And it's funny because
when you're dropping out,

00:08:40.104 --> 00:08:44.538
what you imagine the glory of, okay I'm
leaving school, this is going to be so

00:08:44.538 --> 00:08:48.441
glorious, think about all the people
that dropped out before us.

00:08:48.441 --> 00:08:53.345
You have Bill Gates, Bill Gates dropped
out and this is going to be so exciting.

00:08:53.345 --> 00:08:57.940
And then, you've got Steve Jobs and
he dropped out and this is super exciting,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.301
and Michael Dell dropped out and
that's super exciting,

00:09:01.301 --> 00:09:03.853
but nobody ever really remembers this guy.

00:09:03.853 --> 00:09:06.455
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so we always sort of think about

00:09:06.455 --> 00:09:10.886
the glorious stories of dropping out,
but actually, I don't even know this guy

00:09:10.886 --> 00:09:14.562
dropped out, but it looks like he
must have or had to do something.

00:09:14.562 --> 00:09:18.343
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But we were so pumped, we were like,

00:09:18.343 --> 00:09:21.478
we're dropping out of college,
**** this, and

00:09:21.478 --> 00:09:24.236
we're going to go start
a software company.

00:09:24.236 --> 00:09:28.796
And we were in this position where,
we were living and working, and

00:09:28.796 --> 00:09:33.930
all together, and we were iterating
on our business, over and over again.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:37.220
We were pivoting almost
every every single day.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:41.844
So if you've ever done a startup, what you
find is like maybe every 24 hours you're

00:09:41.844 --> 00:09:45.203
sort of reflecting on your
business model and your strategy.

00:09:45.203 --> 00:09:47.877
And is it going to work,
do you think it's going to scale,

00:09:47.877 --> 00:09:50.610
are the economics actually
going to play out?

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:56.220
And so, we got to this juncture where we
basically had to say: do we want to be

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.360
a company that serves consumers or

00:09:58.360 --> 00:09:59.960
do we want to be a company
that serves businesses?

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:03.540
And the reason that we got to that
juncture was we had consumers

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.760
that were spending, I think at the time,

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:09.910
I think the most you could spend
on our product was $9.99 a month.

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:14.570
So pretty standard consumer
software payment structure,

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.010
so about ten bucks a month.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:21.538
But what we saw was that companies like
Google, and Facebook, and Apple, and

00:10:21.538 --> 00:10:26.135
Microsoft eventually would be giving
everybody free storage on the internet,

00:10:26.135 --> 00:10:29.497
and that you really wouldn't
want to be spending money for

00:10:29.497 --> 00:10:31.023
storage on the internet.

00:10:31.023 --> 00:10:34.411
And let's maybe as just a quick survey,
who actually pays for

00:10:34.411 --> 00:10:38.150
more storage in one of the storage
solutions that you're using?

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:41.740
Maybe you can get a, wow, okay, all right,
you guys just create a lot of data or

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:43.340
something, what's going on here?

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:44.970
Okay, so who doesn't pay?

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:48.435
Let's just see the, okay, right,
so not a great business model,

00:10:48.435 --> 00:10:52.005
when you only have about maybe 35%, or so,

00:10:52.005 --> 00:10:54.505
of the market that's willing
to upgrade for your solution.

00:10:54.505 --> 00:10:57.215
You want to be able to reach
a broader population than that.

00:10:57.215 --> 00:11:00.163
So what we said was at some point Google,
Facebook, and

00:11:00.163 --> 00:11:04.053
others were going to give away unlimited
space, and it was going to be really,

00:11:04.053 --> 00:11:07.968
really hard to upsell people over time,
so that was on the consumer side.

00:11:07.968 --> 00:11:13.349
On the enterprise side though,
what we realized was, actually, companies

00:11:13.349 --> 00:11:18.566
were spending millions and millions of
dollars on software that we thought

00:11:18.566 --> 00:11:24.410
we could do with at a smaller amount of
spend and for a fraction of the price.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:26.740
So companies on one hand
were spending millions or

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:27.870
tens of millions of dollars, and

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:31.240
we thought we could deliver it to them for
maybe hundreds of thousands of dollars.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:32.960
So you could have almost a ten or

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:36.680
20x improvement on sort of
the cost of the service.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:37.720
On the other hand,

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:41.600
we felt that at most we could charge
consumers maybe $10 per month.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:44.810
And that would actually have
downward pressure over time,

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:48.550
just because of the economics of the space
where storage is getting cheaper.

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:52.640
So, it actually took us a surprising
amount of time to come to the conclusion

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.210
that we needed to go in the enterprise
because we still were like, well yeah, but

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:59.750
what if we could serve advertising
on the files, or something?

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:03.420
And it just turns out that, you really
don't want to see ads when you're

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:07.160
looking at your tax documents,
or your photos.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:11.097
And so, we didn't think that the
advertising model was going to work for

00:12:11.097 --> 00:12:11.839
consumers.

00:12:11.839 --> 00:12:15.390
But we did decide that, actually,
if you could build really,

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:19.544
really great enterprise software that
could solve all of the enterprises

00:12:19.544 --> 00:12:23.701
problems around managing information and
collaborating around files.

00:12:23.701 --> 00:12:26.488
And you can build one platform for
being able to do that,

00:12:26.488 --> 00:12:30.344
that maybe you could actually compress
some of the economics in the market and

00:12:30.344 --> 00:12:33.620
be fairly disruptive to
the traditional players.

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:37.060
But we sort of stepped back and we said,
okay, if we're going to do this,

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.560
are we really going to build
an enterprise software company?

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:45.280
We were 23 and 22 at the time,
the founding team, and

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:46.630
we had a few other employees.

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:50.166
But, it's really like, there actually
like a long list of things that you could

00:12:50.166 --> 00:12:53.338
do that aren't very cool, like
somewhere near the bottom of that list,

00:12:53.338 --> 00:12:56.624
when you're 23 and 22, is starting
an enterprise software company.

00:12:56.624 --> 00:13:01.748
So literally, if you just had a quadrant,
a McKinsey quadrant,

00:13:01.748 --> 00:13:08.470
of the lamous thing possible to do, it's
starting an enterprise software company.

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:13.208
So we were pretty sort of
distraught of this idea,

00:13:13.208 --> 00:13:17.320
we were really going to go and build
a company, like an Oracle or an SAP or

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:18.730
something, is that really going to be fun?

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:25.480
Because think about what we think about,
when I say the words enterprise and B2B,

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:30.035
what you should be thinking of, if you
have some experience in the spaces, you

00:13:30.035 --> 00:13:34.835
should be thinking like, okay, it's slow
companies that don't innovate quickly.

00:13:34.835 --> 00:13:39.425
The technologies way to expensive,
like you're just selling to the buyer,

00:13:39.425 --> 00:13:41.475
you're not really selling to the user.

00:13:41.475 --> 00:13:44.265
The technology itself is super complex, so

00:13:44.265 --> 00:13:47.555
there's really no incentive structure to
make really simple enterprise software.

00:13:47.555 --> 00:13:50.785
The reason for that is that because the
people who have the budget don't actually

00:13:50.785 --> 00:13:53.820
have to deal with the complexity of
the technology, so they don't actually

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:58.300
prioritize simplicity and user experience
when they're making buying decisions.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.090
You have all this, you have to deal
with frustrating sales reps, so

00:14:01.090 --> 00:14:03.580
if you're on the other end of
buying enterprise software,

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:07.100
what ends up happening is you're just
getting called all day long by sales reps,

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:10.150
and for
software that you basically don't want.

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:13.530
There's unfriendly sort of policy terms,
so

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:19.090
it's always sort of terms of services that
favor the vendor, not favor the customer.

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:22.570
So the very structure of the contracts

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:26.610
in this industry are totally
not favorable to the client.

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:28.280
Software design is pretty horrible,

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:32.940
because again, if you don't have a market
that is prioritizing user experience or

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:36.860
simplicity, then you can't get the best
designers, because the best designers

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:39.980
don't want to work at companies that
aren't building really simple software.

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:44.564
And so, then what's the point
of having great design?

00:14:44.564 --> 00:14:46.802
And so, then the design ends up sucking.

00:14:46.802 --> 00:14:50.421
You get really infrequent updates, so
it turns out when you sell to enterprises,

00:14:50.421 --> 00:14:53.930
this actually makes sense,
enterprises want a lot of predictability.

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:57.670
They don't want their technology changing
on them at the same rate that Snapchat or

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:59.258
Facebook is able to change
their software for us.

00:14:59.258 --> 00:15:04.185
Because if I'm a CIO,
Chief Information Officer, of a 20 or

00:15:04.185 --> 00:15:07.180
50,000 person company, and my software
vendor is changing all the time,

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.550
that's going to make it so
I have to go retrain my employees.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:13.940
And so, what that then means is that
the vendors, the software vendors, don't

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:16.610
want to update their software and don't
want to update their technology a lot,

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.370
because the customers want
to be infrequently updated.

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:21.808
And so, that then slows down
all of your innovation.

00:15:21.808 --> 00:15:26.000
Imagine working at a company where you
ship product updates maybe once a year, or

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.050
once every three years,
that's pretty annoying, so

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:29.860
you're not going to get
the best innovation.

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:31.270
You have all this downtime,

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:34.622
just cause it's like really creaky
technology that always has like

00:15:34.622 --> 00:15:38.762
these wierd problems that you run into,
corrupted data, so these are the things.

00:15:38.762 --> 00:15:42.141
The list could be about five times longer,
if you were to make a list,

00:15:42.141 --> 00:15:45.291
of like what words describe
the enterprise software industry,

00:15:45.291 --> 00:15:47.540
this is just sort of getting started.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:50.291
And it all sort of comes,
if you kind of took it all together,

00:15:50.291 --> 00:15:52.172
and you sort of rounded it all together.

00:15:52.172 --> 00:15:55.357
Basically like the picture that
people have in their head,

00:15:55.357 --> 00:15:58.479
when you say enterprise software
is basically like this,

00:15:58.479 --> 00:16:01.200
it's just your vendor
just being angry at you.

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:02.460
Actually, it looks a little
bit more like this,

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:04.150
it's a little bit more pirate-oriented.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:07.320
And this is basically what

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:10.930
enterprises think of when they think
of enterprise software companies.

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:12.480
When they think about
enterprise technology vendors,

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.440
at least ten years ago.

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:18.036
So we said, okay, if we're going to
pivot the company and we're going to

00:16:18.036 --> 00:16:22.388
forego all the fun, and all the exciting
times we would have as a consumer company.

00:16:22.388 --> 00:16:26.986
Then we have to find a way to build
an enterprise software company that

00:16:26.986 --> 00:16:31.750
operates and acts and innovates and
moves like a consumer company.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:34.991
If we're going to forgo the consumer side,
and

00:16:34.991 --> 00:16:38.405
serving just consumers
in their personal lives.

00:16:38.405 --> 00:16:42.780
We still have to find a way to build a
company that feels like a consumer company

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.220
that just happens to sell to enterprises.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:50.640
And so then we made a list of basically,
what are the things that are changing in

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:56.150
the technology world that allow for you to
build a company in a very different way?

00:16:56.150 --> 00:17:00.021
And this is, I think what makes enterprise
software so exciting for all of us today.

00:17:00.021 --> 00:17:04.188
Which is why it might not still be
the number one coolest thing to

00:17:04.188 --> 00:17:08.770
do of all time, but it's at least
getting in the top 300 options.

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:13.050
So, we said, okay, if we're going to build
a different enterprise software company,

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:14.520
what is true today?

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:17.942
And this is back in 2006 and
2007, what is true in 2006 and

00:17:17.942 --> 00:17:21.668
7 that allows us to build a different
kind of enterprise software company?

00:17:21.668 --> 00:17:23.300
Well the first is,
let's look at the old way.

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:26.440
So the old way was,
you had really complex technology.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:30.845
It was all about the sales,
what was your distribution channel.

00:17:30.845 --> 00:17:34.630
So you had to have salespeople as your
primary way into getting into customers.

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:37.580
Because customers couldn't access
your technology on their own,

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:38.740
they had to install it.

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:41.169
To install it, you had to have
a salesperson go out and try and

00:17:41.169 --> 00:17:43.371
pitch to the customer that
they should start using it.

00:17:43.371 --> 00:17:48.155
And so all of your distribution was
constrained by the number of sales reps

00:17:48.155 --> 00:17:49.092
that you had.

00:17:49.092 --> 00:17:52.220
Technology was only available
to the biggest companies.

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:53.890
And so
this ends up being a pretty big flop,

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:58.580
because if you're trying to go have
a massive impact on the world.

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:03.220
But only the Fortune 500 can actually
afford to use your technology.

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.913
Because nobody else has
the infrastructure, or

00:18:05.913 --> 00:18:10.256
the apparatus to be able to serve large,
to be able to install the technology.

00:18:10.256 --> 00:18:13.022
Then really you're not having that
big of an impact on the world,

00:18:13.022 --> 00:18:16.210
you're just having a big impact on
the top enterprises that are out there.

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:22.670
We saw a lot of flaws with how technology
sort of solved the security problem.

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:27.320
So, when you buy enterprise software ten
years ago, you bought the infrastructure,

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:28.410
or the hardware.

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:29.654
Then you bought software,

00:18:29.654 --> 00:18:33.126
then you bought security software to
secure the software and the hardware.

00:18:33.126 --> 00:18:37.092
[SOUND] Bless you, and so you had all
this technology that you had to have,

00:18:37.092 --> 00:18:39.121
buy from lots of different vendors.

00:18:39.121 --> 00:18:41.700
And it was the customer's responsibility
for putting it all together.

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:45.290
And so that meant that there was a lot
of security vulnerability inside of

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:46.300
those platforms.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:48.800
There's also a thing where
the customer's bearing the risk.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:52.298
Because what happens is the customer
is buying the licenses for

00:18:52.298 --> 00:18:55.274
the software up front,
regardless of what they use.

00:18:55.274 --> 00:18:58.280
And so you have this sort of asymmetry
where the vendor always wins because

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:01.480
the customer is buying the software
in perpetuity essentially.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:06.305
And what's happening is even if
the software doesn't work for them,

00:19:06.305 --> 00:19:08.910
the customer's already paid.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:12.890
So you'd see all the time, you'd have
these software projects that enterprises

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:17.320
would have where they'd spend $10 million
on software and they'd never install it.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.155
Because, it wasn't what they wanted and
there's no recourse.

00:19:20.155 --> 00:19:22.615
Once they bought it they
can't do anything about it.

00:19:22.615 --> 00:19:26.725
So pretty fundamental
broken business model.

00:19:26.725 --> 00:19:30.165
And then finally, the platforms
themselves were fairly proprietary.

00:19:30.165 --> 00:19:33.325
So they were closed systems,
they weren't integrated and

00:19:33.325 --> 00:19:36.825
open to other platforms, and so
it was very proprietary technology.

00:19:36.825 --> 00:19:40.860
And so we said well, what if there's
a new version of each of these things?

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:44.531
What if you could make it so
the technology was simple and elegant?

00:19:44.531 --> 00:19:46.914
And we always focused on the end user, and

00:19:46.914 --> 00:19:50.020
we never lost sight of
the power of the end user?

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:55.830
What if the product was actually pulled
into organizations by those end users,

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:58.930
as opposed to you having to
only do tops down in sales?

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.870
What if the software was so good and so

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:05.180
freely available that anyone could bring
it into the workplace on their own?

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:10.099
What if it was available to people
inside of organizations of all sizes.

00:20:10.099 --> 00:20:12.720
What if it could be a small
business anywhere around the world?

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.145
What if it could be a large enterprise?

00:20:14.145 --> 00:20:15.630
What if it could be
a three person startup?

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:19.883
So how could you make that technology
available to anybody of all size?

00:20:19.883 --> 00:20:23.914
How could you make sure that security was
baked in, so the customer doesn't have to

00:20:23.914 --> 00:20:27.090
buy all this other technology and
wrap it around these tools.

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:29.960
What if the vendor could
bear more of the risk?

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:31.590
So if you're a customer of ours and

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:33.980
the technology is not working,
you're not going to renew.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.734
So what if more of that risk to making
sure you're successful is actually

00:20:37.734 --> 00:20:39.958
something that the vendor has to focus on?

00:20:39.958 --> 00:20:41.430
And then finally,
what if you had to open systems?

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:45.282
What if you connected to all the different
enterprise software that people were

00:20:45.282 --> 00:20:48.571
using, and you didn't have a closed
platform that locked in data?

00:20:48.571 --> 00:20:49.781
So we kind of said, okay,

00:20:49.781 --> 00:20:53.480
there's this old way that the Oracles
of the world built their technology.

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:56.340
And then there's a new way, and
what if we could describe each

00:20:56.340 --> 00:20:59.319
of these new things that was
different about technology today?

00:20:59.319 --> 00:21:03.225
And go build an enterprise software
company that really solved this core set

00:21:03.225 --> 00:21:03.850
of issues.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.970
And that's essentially what we've
been working on for the past decade.

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:10.680
So we said, here are the new
conditions of this landscape.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:15.247
Let's build a software company for
the enterprise that is built and

00:21:15.247 --> 00:21:21.272
operates like a consumer company, and
that's been our strategy since about 2007.

00:21:21.272 --> 00:21:26.171
And fortunately, what's cool is that
it has played out in favor of a lot of

00:21:26.171 --> 00:21:30.530
the things that we've been talking
about or that we identified.

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:33.270
So we now have about 71,000 customers.

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:36.083
We're in about 63% of the Fortune 500.

00:21:36.083 --> 00:21:40.479
And this was generated mostly because
people just at work wanted to bring in

00:21:40.479 --> 00:21:41.900
better technology.

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:45.232
And then we eventually were able to
sell to the broader organization.

00:21:45.232 --> 00:21:47.680
But it was really about solving for
that user.

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:49.680
Pulling the technology into the workplace,
and

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:52.270
then ultimately solving a bunch
of problems from there.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:55.230
And the exciting thing about what

00:21:55.230 --> 00:22:00.180
has played out in the cloud is that you
have companies of all sizes and in all

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:04.130
industries that are now transforming
the way they work, because of the cloud.

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:07.888
And so we can work with companies
as wide ranging as Pixar,

00:22:07.888 --> 00:22:12.351
where they use Box to be able to share and
collaborate around movies.

00:22:12.351 --> 00:22:17.980
To Nike, where they use Box for
product and marketing.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:22.656
All the way to Eli Lilly where
you have things like R&amp;D and

00:22:22.656 --> 00:22:27.736
drug discovery and
various medical discoveries happening.

00:22:27.736 --> 00:22:29.960
And collaborating around
through Box as well.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:32.790
So what's cool is,
it doesn't matter the industry.

00:22:32.790 --> 00:22:38.290
Companies of all sizes have realized all
of this potential for moving to the cloud.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:43.843
And we are one of many companies that
are benefitting from this set of trends.

00:22:43.843 --> 00:22:46.524
And the cool thing, I think,
for all of us and for

00:22:46.524 --> 00:22:49.406
anybody starting a company
in this space is really,

00:22:49.406 --> 00:22:53.550
this is just the beginning of a whole
significant change in this industry.

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:56.796
So going back ten years,
I thought it was the beginning but

00:22:56.796 --> 00:23:00.454
it was still pretty slow start to
kind of get to where we are today.

00:23:00.454 --> 00:23:04.584
But if I look at all of the trends
that are happening in the enterprise,

00:23:04.584 --> 00:23:08.590
I think the sort of best opportunities
are actually yet to come.

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:09.450
And so

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:13.100
this means there's going to be more growth
and more opportunity than ever before.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:17.060
And the reason for this is
basically you have two big factors.

00:23:17.060 --> 00:23:20.470
First is, most of the technology
still used by enterprises

00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:22.420
is still that legacy technology.

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:25.160
It's still old, it's slow,
it's complex, it's expensive,

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:26.540
it's sold by the legacy vendors.

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:31.120
And so on one hand you just have
this gigantic market of old stuff

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:34.920
that is going to have to get retired,
shut down, and moved to the cloud.

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:36.104
That's the first thing.

00:23:36.104 --> 00:23:40.830
But the other trend is that every business
on the planet is recognizing that that

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:45.274
legacy technology is not going to let
them transform their business models,

00:23:45.274 --> 00:23:47.759
the way that they serve their customers.

00:23:47.759 --> 00:23:52.507
The way that they operate and you're
probably familiar with the fact that

00:23:52.507 --> 00:23:57.409
every single company on the planet at
this point knows they need to modernize

00:23:57.409 --> 00:24:00.411
their organization in
some significant way.

00:24:00.411 --> 00:24:03.970
So if you're a media company You're
freaking out because of Netflix.

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:07.820
If you're a life sciences company,
you're freaking out because of 23andMe.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:11.310
If you're a financial services company,
you're freaking out because of Stripe.

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:14.640
If you're a hospitality company,
you're freaking out because of Airbnb.

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:19.620
So every single company, in every single
industry recognizes that you have

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:24.870
all of these disruptive forces coming
from the digital industries and

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:28.070
Silicon Valley and
broader than Silicon Valley.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:31.010
And that every single company
is going to have to operate and

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:33.390
upgrade both the technology that they use,

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:35.880
as well as the sort of business
models that they have.

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:38.650
And what's cool is if you're building for
enterprises,

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:42.340
that means there's an unbelievable amount
of opportunity because all those companies

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:45.570
All the Fortune 500 and all the other
companies that need to be transforming

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:50.470
are going to need modern software to be
able to work and operate in this new way.

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:53.600
They're going to need new
technology that allows them

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:59.230
to be able to actually compete
effectively with the digital economy.

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:03.910
So that means for all of us that there's
more opportunity than ever before.

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:10.070
Because if I go back ten years ago, there
wasn't actually a significant catalyst for

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:14.250
enterprises to go upgrade their IT
systems and their technology systems.

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:19.410
But today, that catalyst is just the fact
that every company is being disrupted

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:22.460
in sort of every single way
that they look at the market.

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:27.450
And so that just means that you have this
cowless which is If I'm a General Electric

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:31.400
or I'm a Walmart or I'm a Proctor and
Gamble, I need to be working with start

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:34.790
ups and new suppliers that are going to
make me more effective and more

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:38.870
competitive, which means that then there's
opportunity if you're building for B2B.

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:42.660
So if I can convince you to
build an enterprise software

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:45.050
company here are some
lessons that we have learned.

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:49.350
And a lot of these actually
apply probably for most markets.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:53.550
The ones that we've specifically taken
from building from the B2B market but

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:57.665
certainly a lot of these apply whether
you're building a consumer app or

00:25:57.665 --> 00:26:01.890
a medical device company or
any kind of entrepreneurial activity.

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:06.830
But here are sort of I think eight lessons
that we've taken from our journey.

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:10.870
And a few that I've learned from other
companies over the years as well

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:12.160
that I'll try and weave in.

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:16.521
So some of the things that
we've been thinking about.

00:26:16.521 --> 00:26:22.988
The first is, and this one has just been
the foundational way that we got started,

00:26:22.988 --> 00:26:26.928
which is start with something
insanely simple and

00:26:26.928 --> 00:26:30.680
then focus on expanding it over time.

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:36.990
When we got started,
if you were in enterprise IT or

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:41.500
you worked at one of our competitors
like Microsoft or SAP or at Oracle.

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:45.310
And you looked at our software you
literally would have died laughing.

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:47.070
You would have looked
at our technology and

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:50.720
you would be like that is
the stupidest thing I've ever seen,

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:54.030
like all it does is you upload
the file and the you access the file.

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:58.460
And it was that simplicity that was
actually why it was so disruptive,

00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:03.460
because all of those vendors thought
that the problem was we just need to jam

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:08.330
in as many features as humanly possible
into one piece of software, and

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:10.930
that was correlated with
our value proposition.

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:15.187
That was sort of how enterprise
software companies operated.

00:27:15.187 --> 00:27:18.160
Mostly because we weren't
focused on the enterprise.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:20.230
We just took the opposite approach,

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:24.371
we said actually the fewer features,
the simpler set of functionality in

00:27:24.371 --> 00:27:27.463
one place is actually going to
be our value proposition.

00:27:27.463 --> 00:27:30.040
And If you were in classic enterprise
software, you would have looked

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:32.485
at our product and you would have
thought it was a toy essentially,

00:27:32.485 --> 00:27:35.279
you would have been like, that is never
going to serve enterprises, it's not

00:27:35.279 --> 00:27:38.500
going to serve regulated businesses, it's
not going to serve complex work flows.

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:41.815
And what they didn't recognize
was that was just the start,

00:27:41.815 --> 00:27:43.780
that was just our day one use case.

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:45.880
That was the day one thing
we were trying to solve.

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:50.680
So, the instinct and the tendency,
and this still happens today

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:54.800
like at our company 12 years later when
we've learned this, I think so directly.

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:57.870
You still have this tendency,
anybody in this room that goes out and

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:00.992
brainstorms, how can we build
a big enterprise software company?

00:28:00.992 --> 00:28:04.732
You're going to brainstorm by listing
out lots and lots and lots of things.

00:28:04.732 --> 00:28:06.550
You're going to list out lots and
lots and lots of features,

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:09.750
like this wish list of what if you
could just build all this cool ****?

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:11.428
But that is not actually how
you are going to be successful.

00:28:11.428 --> 00:28:13.699
You are going to be successful
by nailing one use case

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:19.280
that just happens to be a use case that
everybody has a massive problem with.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:22.810
And you can nail that one use case,
that becomes essentially the wedge

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:27.120
to then use to expand out to all the other
use cases that you want to go and solve.

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:30.825
And so you have to sort of resist
the natural instinct, which is that more

00:28:30.825 --> 00:28:35.263
functionality, more powerful software is
better, and really make sure that you're

00:28:35.263 --> 00:28:39.430
focused on one thing that you're
going to do better than everybody else.

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:43.610
A great example that I've been
re-reminded of this over and

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:48.300
over from is a company called Gusto
that was previously called Zen Payroll.

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:52.300
And what they did was,
they just said that payroll software and

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:56.550
payroll services sucks in small
businesses, as an example.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:59.810
And so, they built a software
application that was just about

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:03.470
making sure your employees get
paid as efficiently as possible.

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:07.454
And then eventually that was so
successful, and they got embedded into so

00:29:07.454 --> 00:29:12.016
many businesses that they could eventually
expand to more and more HR services, and

00:29:12.016 --> 00:29:15.631
more and more things that an HR team
has to do with their employees.

00:29:15.631 --> 00:29:19.490
But they started with that one use case
which is, let's just get payroll right.

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:21.850
Make it so successful, so simple, so

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:24.460
effective that then you
can build up over time.

00:29:24.460 --> 00:29:26.030
So again, when you're starting out,

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:30.080
you have to resist the urge to want
to build a lot of functionality.

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:33.150
And really focus on one use case
that people are going to be able to

00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:36.040
easily describe to other people and
then be able to go from there.

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:39.185
So that's the first lesson,
and again 12 years in,

00:29:39.185 --> 00:29:42.600
we're still having to relearn
this over and over again,

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:45.860
the amount of product meetings that I end
up in, where we have to be like, we have

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:49.720
to remove that feature, we have to hide
that thing, we have to make that simpler.

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:50.800
It never goes away.

00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:53.350
Even when you're bigger,
you still have to make sure you're really,

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:58.130
really focused on keeping things
dead simple and almost deceptively

00:29:58.130 --> 00:30:01.590
simple in what you're actually
going to go and do for the customer.

00:30:01.590 --> 00:30:06.131
Because it just turns out that in these
cases, having less functionality gives

00:30:06.131 --> 00:30:11.031
people way more opportunity than go use
your service, and it's counterintuitive.

00:30:11.031 --> 00:30:14.720
But the more functionality you have,
the more you're going to narrow the set of

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:18.352
customers that ultimately are going to be
able to make sense of your product and

00:30:18.352 --> 00:30:19.870
want to use your product.

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:21.392
That's the first thing.

00:30:21.392 --> 00:30:26.660
The other major factor is,
and I think that

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:29.850
as you're starting your company you
really need to think hard about this one,

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:36.030
which is make sure that you're benefiting
from a major technology tailwind.

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:40.280
There are so many companies that
get started where there's no

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:46.060
particular macro technology trend
that they're riding on top of.

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:50.836
And in some cases even worse, there could
be technology trends that you're sort

00:30:50.836 --> 00:30:54.105
of resisting, or
that are points of friction for you.

00:30:54.105 --> 00:30:58.513
And so you really have to think hard
about what is going on in the technology

00:30:58.513 --> 00:31:02.864
landscape that I am going to be able
to ride that wave with my innovation.

00:31:02.864 --> 00:31:06.585
Where the growth of whatever
that technology trend is,

00:31:06.585 --> 00:31:09.610
is something that I can
then grow alongside.

00:31:09.610 --> 00:31:13.545
In our case, we benefitted from three or
four megatrends.

00:31:13.545 --> 00:31:15.543
We benefitted from the growth of mobile.

00:31:15.543 --> 00:31:18.150
We benefited from the lower
cost of computing.

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:21.640
We benefitted from the fact that
everybody was coming onto the Internet.

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:25.950
And all three of those trends
were disruptive to our incumbents

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:28.230
that were selling traditional
enterprise software.

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:33.267
And so we were able to take these
megatrends that we had nothing to do with,

00:31:33.267 --> 00:31:38.467
take these mega trends, ride on top of
them, and they were just an onslaught

00:31:38.467 --> 00:31:43.290
of basically disaster for
the traditional competitors in the space.

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:47.388
What are the technology trends that you
think incumbents are not going to be

00:31:47.388 --> 00:31:52.294
able to respond well to, because they're
sort of economically, strategically, or

00:31:52.294 --> 00:31:53.840
from a talent standpoint,

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:58.380
not able to leverage those technology
trends in the same way that you can?

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:00.400
PlanGrade is a pretty
cool example of this.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:04.230
They basically do software For
construction companies and for

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:05.202
architecture firms.

00:32:05.202 --> 00:32:08.060
And builders to be able to
let you do blueprints and

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:12.370
other kind of construction projects
on the iPad and the iPhone.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:16.310
And and it's a pretty dead simple idea.

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:19.980
But it's going after just the fact that
there's billions of dollars spent every

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:24.020
single year on either software
that is stuck on your computer or

00:32:24.020 --> 00:32:25.550
paper based processes.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:30.330
And they could basically ride
the growth of mobile and the growth of

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:35.190
mobile in the sort of field worker
scenarios to be able to grow with that.

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:38.960
So they nailed the timing perfectly of,
okay,

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:40.770
it was a couple years after the iPad.

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:43.430
Now's the time where these devices
are going to start to show up in

00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:44.920
physical environments.

00:32:44.920 --> 00:32:48.980
What is the software that we need to do
to be able to let construction teams and

00:32:48.980 --> 00:32:52.210
building companies be able
to use that technology?

00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:56.300
So really make sure that you're
going to benefit significantly

00:32:56.300 --> 00:33:00.210
from a major tailwind, and don't create
unnecessary headwinds for yourself.

00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:08.070
Your product, in a perfect world,
should really literally sell itself.

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:11.190
And this is, again, when you're
going out and you're building for

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:16.510
the enterprise, instinctively you're not
thinking about how can I make the product

00:33:16.510 --> 00:33:18.740
be that sort of point of viral adoption?

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:21.493
Because we're so
used to sort of building up sales teams.

00:33:21.493 --> 00:33:25.564
And building up all the sort of customer
relationships, which are still important,

00:33:25.564 --> 00:33:29.463
because you're still going to have to use
probably sales to ultimately close large

00:33:29.463 --> 00:33:30.805
deals with clients.

00:33:30.805 --> 00:33:35.668
But make sure that the product itself
is something that can be adopted,

00:33:35.668 --> 00:33:39.813
spread, and utilized without
any friction by the end user.

00:33:39.813 --> 00:33:44.955
Sales really should be used to close big
deals, not to drive product adoption.

00:33:44.955 --> 00:33:48.762
And that's a big distinction in
the traditional enterprise software world,

00:33:48.762 --> 00:33:49.626
where sales was,

00:33:49.626 --> 00:33:53.400
sort of the only way that you could push
out product adoption into companies.

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:58.090
And so in our case,
what we did was we put a huge

00:33:58.090 --> 00:34:02.760
emphasis on all of our
sharing functionality in Box.

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:06.050
So when you had a document that you wanted
to share, we wanted to make it really,

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:09.290
really, really simple to be able
to share those files around.

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:11.620
So then the software would spread itself,

00:34:11.620 --> 00:34:14.740
as opposed to us having to just
keep selling it into companies

00:34:14.740 --> 00:34:17.720
like the traditional enterprise
software vendors were doing.

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:20.410
And then you use sales
to close bigger deals,

00:34:20.410 --> 00:34:24.320
essentially to help sort of navigate
in that customer environment.

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:28.320
And so, Slack today is sort of the perfect
quintessential example of a viral

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:33.320
enterprise software product where the very
usage of the service is to then spread

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:36.990
to other people because by definition,
you're communicating with other people.

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:41.520
And they've really done this
extremely well, which is make sure

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:46.480
that you're building a product that is as
easy to adopt and as viral as possible.

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:49.320
Every single thing about
that product is tuned for

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:52.960
the end-user to be able to be
empowered to spread it on their own.

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:55.670
So they can build their own sort
of viral distribution channel.

00:34:55.670 --> 00:34:57.730
Sales then comes in later.

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:00.990
And then you can sell additional services
or capabilities to that enterprise.

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:05.640
And so you really want to have
a bifurcation between product adoption and

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:09.810
sales, making sure you're
doing the bigger deals, okay.

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:17.660
Next one, spend time, a lot of time,
with your sort of futuristic customers,

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:21.220
but don't exactly just build
what they're asking for.

00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:24.740
And so what happens when your building for
the enterprise, you will always have,

00:35:24.740 --> 00:35:30.720
inevitably, you will always
have a bell curve of customers.

00:35:30.720 --> 00:35:36.196
Of sort of how innovative, or
how leaning into the future they are,

00:35:36.196 --> 00:35:39.390
versus how conservative they are.

00:35:39.390 --> 00:35:44.090
It's the customers that are sort of
at the first, sort of 5 to 10% of

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:47.770
that bell curve of, they're at
the bleeding edge of everything.

00:35:47.770 --> 00:35:52.319
These are the people that wait in line and
they get the AirPods.

00:35:52.319 --> 00:35:56.490
And they have an Amazon Alexa
at their desk in their office.

00:35:56.490 --> 00:36:00.340
These are the customers that you
want to spend a lot of time with.

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:04.140
Because they're going to see
things even before you will

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:06.610
about how your technology could
be used in their environment.

00:36:06.610 --> 00:36:11.820
They're going to spot trends of what's
happening in their employee base,

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:16.580
and how they want to push your technology
farther than you even would have imagined

00:36:16.580 --> 00:36:17.986
would have been possible.

00:36:17.986 --> 00:36:20.480
The amount of sort of innovation

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:23.870
that we've been able to drive because of
what we've heard from those customers,

00:36:23.870 --> 00:36:27.600
is probably a significant percentage
of what we've ended up building.

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:31.980
But the difference is from legacy
enterprise software companies to today.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:33.870
Is back in the day what you would do,

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:37.470
is you would just ask them what do you
want us to build, and then you'd go build

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:40.340
those things because that would
essentially lead to a sale or an upsell.

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:47.130
What you need to do is listen, to what
are the issues that they're seeing?

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.530
What are the things they
wish your product could do?

00:36:49.530 --> 00:36:52.716
And then, either work with them,
or come back with solutions,

00:36:52.716 --> 00:36:55.135
that maybe aren't exactly
what they asked for.

00:36:55.135 --> 00:36:57.932
Maybe aren't the specific
features that they asked for, but

00:36:57.932 --> 00:37:00.727
are going to eventually get them
down the path that, really,

00:37:00.727 --> 00:37:02.630
they're trying to push your product in.

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:05.140
So we spend a lot of time
listening to customers.

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:07.520
But not building exactly
what they ask for.

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:10.460
Because when you just build
what a customer asks for,

00:37:10.460 --> 00:37:13.690
essentially you become this sort of
amalgamation of feature requests

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:15.130
from every single one of your customers.

00:37:15.130 --> 00:37:18.272
And there's no way that you're going to
then be able to keep a consistent

00:37:18.272 --> 00:37:21.465
north star with your product, you're
going to be sort of pulled in lots of

00:37:21.465 --> 00:37:23.991
different directions by lots
of different customers.

00:37:23.991 --> 00:37:27.880
But the software vendors that do it
really wrong is by not listening

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:29.770
at all ro those early adoptors.

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:32.820
They start focusing on the most
conservative companies.

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:36.145
They start focusing on the people
that will only adopt if

00:37:36.145 --> 00:37:39.080
they built all of the features
that they ask for.

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:42.025
And that's really how you go wrong in
enterprise software, because then you

00:37:42.025 --> 00:37:45.030
really lose sight of your vision and
ultimately what you're trying to build.

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:49.980
And so we have a bunch of different
groups that we work with to try to

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:54.036
learn all of the things that
the customer would want.

00:37:54.036 --> 00:37:56.998
So we have lots of advisory boards and
we're getting lists of hundreds and

00:37:56.998 --> 00:37:58.410
hundreds of requests.

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:00.834
But we don't just go and
build whatever those requests are.

00:38:00.834 --> 00:38:02.678
We spend a lot of time thinking about,

00:38:02.678 --> 00:38:06.140
how can we connect the dots between
multiple requests or multiple ideas

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:09.907
to build a solution that people didn't
really even think about previously?

00:38:09.907 --> 00:38:14.582
Also another area where software companies
go wrong is, you're going to get a lot of

00:38:14.582 --> 00:38:18.840
requests from customers that
are sort of contradictory in nature.

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.150
Somebody in financial
services is going to say,

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:24.620
I want this functionality for my bank.

00:38:24.620 --> 00:38:26.820
And then somebody in life
science is going to say,

00:38:26.820 --> 00:38:29.730
I want this functionality for
my pharmaceutical company.

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:34.030
And you're not going to be able to kind
of actually synchronize those requests.

00:38:34.030 --> 00:38:38.200
They're going to fundamentally
require different user experiences,

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:39.450
different product functionality.

00:38:39.450 --> 00:38:41.580
And where a lot of software
companies go wrong is,

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:44.360
you start to sort of build all
these features in isolation.

00:38:44.360 --> 00:38:47.600
And then you sort of zoom
out five years later and

00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:50.050
you've just built this massive technology.

00:38:50.050 --> 00:38:53.392
That has, okay we've got this
vertical offering for that industry.

00:38:53.392 --> 00:38:56.120
We've got a separate vertical offering for
that industry.

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:59.668
You can learn a lot from companies like
Salesforce which essentially said,

00:38:59.668 --> 00:39:04.400
okay, we're going to build these modular
components which can be mixed and

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:08.170
matched to create user experience for
different industries, for

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:11.500
different lines of business,
for different job functions.

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:16.170
We're not going to bake in all of the
functionality directly into the product,

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:18.810
we're going to give customers the tools,
or

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:22.130
even our own team to do that
customization themselves.

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:25.922
So a lot of companies get into a trap
where you just are mindlessly building

00:39:25.922 --> 00:39:26.909
lots of features.

00:39:26.909 --> 00:39:30.174
You sort of zoom out a few years later,
and what you have is this mess of serving

00:39:30.174 --> 00:39:33.710
a whole bunch of different industries with
a really, really bad user experience.

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:39.622
The thing that we still
stay true to today.

00:39:39.622 --> 00:39:43.769
And this is where it gets harder and
harder over time, especially as

00:39:43.769 --> 00:39:48.061
the deals get bigger and bigger,
is making sure that at any juncture,

00:39:48.061 --> 00:39:51.264
you're not ever trading off
the user experience for

00:39:51.264 --> 00:39:56.510
some Enterprise feature or some feature
that's going to get you a bigger deal.

00:39:56.510 --> 00:40:00.120
There's going to be hundreds of times
where you're at this moment where

00:40:00.120 --> 00:40:03.978
the customer is asking for
something that is antithetical Or

00:40:03.978 --> 00:40:08.526
going to go against the simplicity
of that user experience.

00:40:08.526 --> 00:40:11.497
Maybe its a security feature,
maybe its a compliance feature,

00:40:11.497 --> 00:40:15.249
maybe it's something that they're just
trying to put in the way of the end user.

00:40:15.249 --> 00:40:17.875
And at every single one
of those decision points,

00:40:17.875 --> 00:40:21.640
we have made sure that we always favor
the end user over the enterprise.

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:25.240
And in some cases,
that means that we will lose deals.

00:40:25.240 --> 00:40:28.574
And so, we'll have to say no to
a customer, because by building that

00:40:28.574 --> 00:40:32.313
feature, we don't think we're going to
be able to deliver the delight, and

00:40:32.313 --> 00:40:35.032
the user experience that we're looking for
over time.

00:40:35.032 --> 00:40:37.069
And so, that gets really really painful,

00:40:37.069 --> 00:40:40.250
because you lose then deals
all throughout the years.

00:40:40.250 --> 00:40:43.410
So if I look back over the past ten years,
we've lost tens

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:48.060
of millions of dollars of business,
because we said no to certain customers.

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:51.468
What we benefited from though,
was a product that many,

00:40:51.468 --> 00:40:55.516
many years later could serve a much
wider population of customers.

00:40:55.516 --> 00:40:59.068
That ended up making our existing
customers much more successful with our

00:40:59.068 --> 00:40:59.590
product.

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:05.910
And funny enough, the companies
that once didn't say yes to us, and

00:41:05.910 --> 00:41:10.250
took their business somewhere else, ended
up being unsuccessful with those products,

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:12.220
and had to eventually come back to us.

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:16.090
And so, if you're right, and the key
point here is you have to be right.

00:41:16.090 --> 00:41:20.255
If you're right and that user trade off
truly was going to be disastrous for

00:41:20.255 --> 00:41:21.775
that user experience, and

00:41:21.775 --> 00:41:25.427
you remain true to that north star
of always focusing on the user.

00:41:25.427 --> 00:41:27.184
Eventually, over the long run and

00:41:27.184 --> 00:41:30.500
sort of long run can be defined
whatever market you're in.

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:33.850
You will end up winning in
that market over the long run.

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:35.750
But you're going to lose
deals along the way and

00:41:35.750 --> 00:41:38.961
that's going to be really stressful for
you, for your team, for investors.

00:41:38.961 --> 00:41:42.483
But it's really important that you're
crystal clear on the principles of how

00:41:42.483 --> 00:41:45.852
you're building your product and
what you're going to say no to.

00:41:45.852 --> 00:41:52.993
Finally, Find ways, and this kind of goes
back to that technology trend thing.

00:41:52.993 --> 00:41:57.560
But try and find ways where you can have

00:41:57.560 --> 00:42:03.660
asymmetric advantages
over the competitors.

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:08.822
And what you'll find is,
that your competitors the big incumbents,

00:42:08.822 --> 00:42:14.344
tend to be locked in pretty significantly
to particular business models.

00:42:14.344 --> 00:42:19.676
Because they in some cases have
tens of thousands of employees that

00:42:19.676 --> 00:42:25.114
are built up to build and to deliver
products in a very specific way.

00:42:25.114 --> 00:42:30.292
And so if you can find points of
differentiation and disruption, that

00:42:30.292 --> 00:42:36.454
they can't respond to, because either it
is too expensive for them to respond to.

00:42:36.454 --> 00:42:40.366
Or strategically, it doesn't make as
much sense for them to respond, or

00:42:40.366 --> 00:42:43.095
they don't have maybe
the talent to respond to it.

00:42:43.095 --> 00:42:47.252
That is how you ultimately remain
disruptive to these bigger companies,

00:42:47.252 --> 00:42:50.279
that's the only way to survive,
as a small start up.

00:42:50.279 --> 00:42:54.688
Amazon Web Services, we take it for
granted now, how easily you can build

00:42:54.688 --> 00:42:58.260
a company and just by writing
some code and deploy it on AWS.

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:01.962
10 years ago, 15 years ago, what you had
to do is you had to buy all these servers,

00:43:01.962 --> 00:43:04.959
you had to put them in data centers,
you had to rack them yourselves.

00:43:04.959 --> 00:43:08.535
You had to connect them all together, and
that would take weeks or months and cost

00:43:08.535 --> 00:43:12.123
in some cases hundreds of thousands of
dollars just to get your start-up going.

00:43:12.123 --> 00:43:15.558
But, that's the benefit to us
as building software companies.

00:43:15.558 --> 00:43:20.240
Think about now the difference if you're
one of the vendors that built that

00:43:20.240 --> 00:43:21.080
hardware.

00:43:21.080 --> 00:43:24.160
And you sold those servers, and
you sold that data center space.

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:26.750
Think about how disruptive
Amazon Web Services was,

00:43:26.750 --> 00:43:29.930
coming out of nowhere, and
having a completely different model.

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:34.740
Where now I don't have to buy servers
anymore because I can just go to Amazon.

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:38.350
Amazon did something that was so
disruptive to the core business model

00:43:38.350 --> 00:43:41.890
of traditional server makers and
software providers.

00:43:41.890 --> 00:43:46.411
That literally those companies to
this day have not even responded.

00:43:46.411 --> 00:43:50.074
12 years later they still have not
responded by being able to deliver these

00:43:50.074 --> 00:43:51.563
capabilities over the cloud,

00:43:51.563 --> 00:43:55.180
because of how disrupted it is
from a business mobile standpoint.

00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:59.323
So, really trying to identify are there
things from a product standpoint,

00:43:59.323 --> 00:44:03.854
that can be highly disruptive to how your
product can be delivered to clients versus

00:44:03.854 --> 00:44:06.205
how incumbent solutions were delivered?

00:44:06.205 --> 00:44:11.493
And I kind of wrapped all of
this up with a Steve Jobs quote.

00:44:11.493 --> 00:44:16.013
It's a requirement that you have a Steve
Jobs quote in any product presentation, so

00:44:16.013 --> 00:44:18.941
I need to meet that quota,
but this is really true, and

00:44:18.941 --> 00:44:21.058
it's especially true in enterprise.

00:44:21.058 --> 00:44:25.160
It's really true in consumer, but it's
even more true in enterprise software.

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:31.100
Because what happens is
enterprises are just asking for

00:44:31.100 --> 00:44:32.940
solutions to all their problems.

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:37.550
But, in many cases, they don't know what
the right solution is, until you come out

00:44:37.550 --> 00:44:39.540
of nowhere with something they never
thought would never be possible.

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:42.609
In our case,
just in our product, literally,

00:44:42.609 --> 00:44:47.618
if you took 100 IT people from 100
different companies 12 years ago.

00:44:47.618 --> 00:44:52.017
And you said draw out the best way for
your company to manage documents,

00:44:52.017 --> 00:44:54.743
manage files, secure their information.

00:44:54.743 --> 00:44:59.440
Not in 100 attempts would our
solution have been designed.

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:03.620
Because everybody was so used to
the technology has to really be complex,

00:45:03.620 --> 00:45:06.050
it has to have all these
interface buttons.

00:45:06.050 --> 00:45:08.475
It has to have all these features for
it to make sense.

00:45:08.475 --> 00:45:13.175
We came in with a product that nobody
would have anticipated because of how dead

00:45:13.175 --> 00:45:14.390
simple it was.

00:45:14.390 --> 00:45:17.620
And so you can't really go
ask your customers just like,

00:45:17.620 --> 00:45:19.260
hey what do you want us to do?

00:45:19.260 --> 00:45:21.800
And then, somehow you're going to
end up with a really great solution.

00:45:21.800 --> 00:45:25.690
You have to really think hard about
what is possible in the future?

00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:28.380
What are the things that maybe your
customers aren't anticipating?

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:30.440
And you have to build that, and then,

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:34.190
essentially have that land
inside of the customer base.

00:45:34.190 --> 00:45:37.620
So really make sure that you're not just
building what customers are asking for,

00:45:37.620 --> 00:45:40.180
because no great technology
has been built that way.

00:45:40.180 --> 00:45:41.114
Last thing that I'll leave you with.

00:45:41.114 --> 00:45:46.157
It's my, I think they're my four
favorite books on building B2B stuff,

00:45:46.157 --> 00:45:47.850
but don't hold me to it.

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:51.270
But here are four good books that
I would highly recommend reading,

00:45:51.270 --> 00:45:53.400
if you're going to build
a B2B software company.

00:45:53.400 --> 00:45:58.400
It's all about, it sort of gets you
into the mindset, and have the frame of

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:03.340
reference of how to think about growth,
disruption, building a new market.

00:46:03.340 --> 00:46:06.830
And then ultimately going out and building
a company can scale in this space.

00:46:06.830 --> 00:46:09.590
So if I had read these
four books 12 years ago,

00:46:09.590 --> 00:46:12.660
I think we would've not had to
learn these lessons so slowly.

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:16.050
So I would highly recommend
checking these out.

00:46:16.050 --> 00:46:20.674
So with that I would just say good luck,
please do not compete with me.

00:46:20.674 --> 00:46:23.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I don't need more competition.

00:46:23.150 --> 00:46:26.670
Do not use my advice
against me in any way.

00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:29.941
And hope to see you in the market,
so take care.

00:46:29.941 --> 00:46:34.857
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:46:34.857 --> 00:46:39.382
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Okay, and

00:46:39.382 --> 00:46:40.550
I don't know what happens next.

00:46:40.550 --> 00:46:41.559
But, what?

00:46:41.559 --> 00:46:43.225
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; I have time.

00:46:43.225 --> 00:46:45.190
I have like five minutes.

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:46.492
But I don't know how it works with people.

00:46:46.492 --> 00:46:49.530
Okay, I can answer questions or
you can leave.

00:46:49.530 --> 00:46:51.730
Either way, okay, yes.

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:58.781
&gt;&gt; So what are some ways that you would
actually suggest [INAUDIBLE] lot company.

00:46:58.781 --> 00:47:03.544
because similar to you when you
were actually back in school that

00:47:03.544 --> 00:47:06.696
may not have had [INAUDIBLE] companies.

00:47:06.696 --> 00:47:07.235
Big on that.

00:47:07.235 --> 00:47:08.099
&gt;&gt; Yeah it's hard.

00:47:08.099 --> 00:47:10.932
because I haven't had to
do what you are asking.

00:47:10.932 --> 00:47:14.629
Which is the question was how do you
discover issues that enterprises

00:47:14.629 --> 00:47:18.146
are having without necessarily
working inside the enterprise?

00:47:18.146 --> 00:47:22.888
You know I think one is you spend a lot
of time with people that everyday they

00:47:22.888 --> 00:47:24.970
are going into the office.

00:47:24.970 --> 00:47:27.120
And they are running into
all of these problems.

00:47:27.120 --> 00:47:28.933
And I would just go
interview like 100 people.

00:47:28.933 --> 00:47:33.204
Just like walk me through your day,
what are all the places where you are less

00:47:33.204 --> 00:47:37.840
effective, less efficient,
having to do repetitive redundant work?

00:47:37.840 --> 00:47:42.480
And don't ask for technology problems,
just listen to where they're spending

00:47:42.480 --> 00:47:47.390
their time and what processes do they
keep repeating over and over again.

00:47:47.390 --> 00:47:50.930
And then you kind of start to imagine
well, how could software go and

00:47:50.930 --> 00:47:51.660
attack that problem?

00:47:51.660 --> 00:47:54.980
How can software attack
the problem of meeting scheduling,

00:47:54.980 --> 00:47:56.840
not a big market but one space.

00:47:56.840 --> 00:48:00.120
How could software attack
the problem of payroll?

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:03.250
How could software attack
the problem of expense reporting?

00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:04.570
All these kind of things.

00:48:04.570 --> 00:48:08.725
Are the drudgery of working
inside of a big company.

00:48:08.725 --> 00:48:12.175
But if you haven't worked in one of those
big companies, you at least have to get as

00:48:12.175 --> 00:48:15.379
much information as you can from people
that do work in those big companies.

00:48:15.379 --> 00:48:19.950
And then you can start to see patterns
of this seems like a really big problem.

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:25.070
And in our case, if you had
interviewed clients or people that

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:28.630
worked in big enterprises 15 years ago and
you said, walk me through your day.

00:48:29.720 --> 00:48:32.969
I think you would have eventually spotted
how much time is wasted when people

00:48:32.969 --> 00:48:36.538
are working with their documents, and
they're trying to share their documents.

00:48:36.538 --> 00:48:38.329
And they're trying to access their
documents from different devices.

00:48:38.329 --> 00:48:40.851
If you really peeled it apart, and
you asked all the right questions,

00:48:40.851 --> 00:48:43.050
you would have actually
discovered this problem.

00:48:43.050 --> 00:48:46.790
We discovered it because of what we were
doing in our personal lives in college.

00:48:46.790 --> 00:48:49.640
But you would have also discovered it
by just understanding where people

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:51.120
are spending time in the work place.

00:48:51.120 --> 00:48:54.124
And then saying hey wait a second that
doesn't make sense, because technology

00:48:54.124 --> 00:48:57.474
today could solve that problem way better
than what they are getting as solutions.

00:48:57.474 --> 00:48:58.580
And so you can just,

00:48:58.580 --> 00:49:02.630
as soon as you find that gap that's
where you know you can build a company.

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:09.684
&gt;&gt; I was wondering like you
mentioned about writing the pathway,

00:49:09.684 --> 00:49:13.852
[INAUDIBLE] what do you think
is the biggest high point now?

00:49:13.852 --> 00:49:19.626
&gt;&gt; Well it's not BitCoin, so
sorry to all the BitCoin startups.

00:49:19.626 --> 00:49:23.520
BitCoin could be up in the top five or
something, but you know, I don't want to

00:49:23.520 --> 00:49:26.889
offend all the BitCoin people, so
I'm going to get like hate mail.

00:49:26.889 --> 00:49:30.715
Can we edit that part out
about my attack on BitCoin?

00:49:30.715 --> 00:49:34.796
So, I'm going to say
a bunch of the trends,

00:49:34.796 --> 00:49:41.573
I don't know how to rank order them but
obviously AR/VR is a big one.

00:49:41.573 --> 00:49:46.340
I think AR/VR is It's in a challenging
spot because we don't have ambiguous

00:49:46.340 --> 00:49:47.860
devices yet.

00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:51.531
And so the question is,
is ARVR like mobile was 12 years ago or

00:49:51.531 --> 00:49:53.694
is it still another five years away.

00:49:53.694 --> 00:49:57.852
The last thing you want to do is
be the mobile start up of 2001,

00:49:57.852 --> 00:49:59.855
because those start ups died.

00:49:59.855 --> 00:50:03.095
So, and it's actually really hard to
predict these things, so I don't have any

00:50:03.095 --> 00:50:06.430
advice on like how do you predict if
it's actually going to happen or not.

00:50:06.430 --> 00:50:10.860
But there was a bunch of mobile companies,
and funny enough Sam

00:50:10.860 --> 00:50:15.380
would have good stories around this
back in 2004, building a mobile company.

00:50:15.380 --> 00:50:18.550
And how hard it was because
you didn't have the iPhone.

00:50:18.550 --> 00:50:21.640
And so before the iPhone,
it really sucked being in mobile.

00:50:21.640 --> 00:50:24.917
And that's kind of the state of things
right now if you're building ARVR,

00:50:24.917 --> 00:50:27.219
which is like,
we know this is probably the future.

00:50:27.219 --> 00:50:29.894
We don't know if we're looking
at the right era of it yet, so

00:50:29.894 --> 00:50:31.790
that would be the one caveat I'd just say.

00:50:31.790 --> 00:50:35.228
It's going to be the future,
we don't know exactly when.

00:50:35.228 --> 00:50:38.630
AI is a massive tech trend.

00:50:38.630 --> 00:50:42.380
It doesn't necessarily benefit
though startups versus incumbent.

00:50:42.380 --> 00:50:44.996
It's going to be more
pervasive than that so

00:50:44.996 --> 00:50:50.003
I don't know how to necessarily use it as
an advantage over an incumbent because

00:50:50.003 --> 00:50:54.810
the incumbents are actually almost
superior in this technology right now.

00:50:54.810 --> 00:50:58.887
Let's see, you can always expect the cost
of computing is just going to continue

00:50:58.887 --> 00:50:59.390
to drop.

00:50:59.390 --> 00:51:01.295
So we thought that was
a trend 12 years ago.

00:51:01.295 --> 00:51:04.977
Well, It hasn't gone away and it's
actually more powerful than ever before.

00:51:04.977 --> 00:51:08.050
But I think there's some pretty cool
things, like as an example, and

00:51:08.050 --> 00:51:10.409
I might have to change my
own even definition of this.

00:51:10.409 --> 00:51:13.160
Of thinking about
the compounding of two trends.

00:51:13.160 --> 00:51:14.030
So for instance,

00:51:14.030 --> 00:51:18.212
AI and computing together are actually
the really powerful trend right now.

00:51:18.212 --> 00:51:22.914
So Google just launched this new
service that does image recognition and

00:51:22.914 --> 00:51:27.912
video recognition, object
recognition inside images and video.

00:51:27.912 --> 00:51:32.232
So just via an API, you can send
Google an image or a video and

00:51:32.232 --> 00:51:34.862
it will tell you what's in that image or
video.

00:51:34.862 --> 00:51:40.522
So that would have taken like 100
engineers five years ago to go build that.

00:51:40.522 --> 00:51:43.402
And now you get it as
a simple API from Google.

00:51:43.402 --> 00:51:45.428
So thinking about, wait a second.

00:51:45.428 --> 00:51:48.923
What happens when you have a bunch of
these new services that are being created

00:51:48.923 --> 00:51:51.770
on our behalf from Google,
Amazon, Microsoft, etc.

00:51:51.770 --> 00:51:55.610
What's the new set of problems that
we could use those solutions and

00:51:55.610 --> 00:51:56.420
build software for.

00:51:56.420 --> 00:52:00.790
So, maybe you build a company
that does security cameras.

00:52:00.790 --> 00:52:03.780
That plugs into Google
computer vision API.

00:52:03.780 --> 00:52:07.866
Maybe you build a new way to navigate or
search for images and

00:52:07.866 --> 00:52:10.000
data because of these API's.

00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:13.828
So, there's a lot of the new problems,
that you wouldn't have even been able

00:52:13.828 --> 00:52:17.366
to see were a problem before you knew
that this technology was possible.

00:52:17.366 --> 00:52:20.417
And so now you can start to look at the
world in a different way if you have kind

00:52:20.417 --> 00:52:24.227
of complete understanding of what all
those API's are that are out there now.

00:52:24.227 --> 00:52:26.610
Maybe the last question, yeah.

00:52:26.610 --> 00:52:29.712
&gt;&gt; I was wonder,
what's your opinion, as a startup,

00:52:29.712 --> 00:52:34.661
using other enterprise softwares on your
platform, or just building up your own?

00:52:34.661 --> 00:52:35.560
&gt;&gt; What's an example?

00:52:35.560 --> 00:52:38.828
&gt;&gt; So, I mean, so
I guess servers are pretty obvious to use,

00:52:38.828 --> 00:52:42.648
but for instances money transactions,
using something for that.

00:52:42.648 --> 00:52:45.205
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Do you have something specific that maybe

00:52:45.205 --> 00:52:46.551
could be built versus.

00:52:46.551 --> 00:52:51.434
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that the question
is when do you decide what to

00:52:51.434 --> 00:52:56.608
build versus what to use other
companies for and I think for us,

00:52:56.608 --> 00:53:03.070
I generally believe that anything
that is not your core competency.

00:53:03.070 --> 00:53:07.048
You need to make sure that you're using
the best in class outside solution for.

00:53:07.048 --> 00:53:10.810
And so, the moment that you realized.

00:53:10.810 --> 00:53:14.568
I'm not going to be the best company at
payments, you better be using Stripe or

00:53:14.568 --> 00:53:16.283
something else for that problem.

00:53:16.283 --> 00:53:18.040
You shouldn't be building that yourself.

00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:21.330
So, the last thing you want to do
is spend any time as a company

00:53:21.330 --> 00:53:25.450
building out things that the market is
going to solve better than you will and

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:28.980
make sure that you are extremely
clear on your core competency.

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:31.350
And I think this is
actually where start-ups go

00:53:31.350 --> 00:53:32.800
sort of the wrong way a lot of times.

00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:36.940
Is not getting crystal clear on
what is the thing that we can do

00:53:36.940 --> 00:53:41.390
that the market is not going to go and
build and commoditize?

00:53:41.390 --> 00:53:42.760
And what's interesting,

00:53:42.760 --> 00:53:47.240
and kind of we've learned this over the
years is that's not a static definition.

00:53:47.240 --> 00:53:48.810
The market is always changing, and so

00:53:48.810 --> 00:53:52.220
your core competency might
actually be changing you know,

00:53:52.220 --> 00:53:56.080
every couple of years, because you have to
adapt with the realities of the market.

00:53:56.080 --> 00:53:57.630
If I got back ten years ago or

00:53:57.630 --> 00:54:00.760
12 years ago,
we had that picture of the four of us.

00:54:00.760 --> 00:54:02.361
We would go into data centers and

00:54:02.361 --> 00:54:05.101
we were building out our
own storage infrastructure.

00:54:05.101 --> 00:54:08.690
And we knew that it sucked that we
were building out our own storage

00:54:08.690 --> 00:54:09.740
infrastructure.

00:54:09.740 --> 00:54:12.800
Because we knew somebody else could
be doing this better than us, but

00:54:12.800 --> 00:54:14.060
there was no other solution.

00:54:14.060 --> 00:54:17.250
Fast forward to today, we have no
problem using Amazon for all of that,

00:54:17.250 --> 00:54:22.670
we have no illusion that that is somehow a
point of differentiation or value for us.

00:54:22.670 --> 00:54:25.102
And so the moment that the rest
of the market catches up and

00:54:25.102 --> 00:54:27.344
commoditizes something that
you used to be good at.

00:54:27.344 --> 00:54:30.850
You need to get away from that part
of the market as fast as possible.

00:54:30.850 --> 00:54:32.268
Cool, all right, I gotta.

00:54:32.268 --> 00:54:32.960
Good luck guys.

00:54:32.960 --> 00:54:34.181
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Take care.

